  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Time Shifting",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a time shift $x(t - t0)$ into the Fourier-domain factor e^(-j $\\omega$ t0) $X(j \\omega)$ so known transform pairs can be reused in transform-property calculations.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Shifting",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A time shift means you should reuse a known Fourier pair instead of recomputing the transform from scratch. Start from a pair like $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, then read the shift sign in the argument to decide which factor to attach.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-t_0) \\leftrightarrow e^{-j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A delay by $t_0$ keeps the original spectrum and multiplies it by $e^{-j\\omega t_0}$. The only change is the shift factor; the base transform pair stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+t_0) \\leftrightarrow e^{+j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "An advance uses the opposite sign in the exponential. Read the sign inside the parentheses first, then match it to the sign in the Fourier factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_{\\text{shifted}}(j\\omega)=e^{-j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the working rule is simple: identify the shift, keep the known spectrum, and attach the correct exponential factor. That is the form you will reuse in the worked examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Delayed Signal from a Known Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $x(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a delayed copy of a known pair. Start from $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and use the delay rule from the tutorial.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-t_0) \\leftrightarrow e^{-j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the delay is $2$, so replace $t_0$ with $2$ and keep the base spectrum unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-2) \\leftrightarrow e^{-j2\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transform of the delayed signal is the original spectrum multiplied by the delay factor $e^{-j2\\omega}$. No new transform integral is needed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming an Advanced Signal from a Known Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $e^{-t}u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}$, find the Fourier transform of $e^{-(t-3)}u(t-3)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a delayed causal exponential. Start from the known pair $e^{-t}u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}$ and recognize the shift in the time argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-t}u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The given signal is the same base signal delayed by $3$, so the time-shift rule attaches $e^{-j3\\omega}$ to the original spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-(t-3)}u(t-3) \\leftrightarrow e^{-j3\\omega}\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transformed signal keeps the denominator $1+j\\omega$ and only picks up the delay factor. The result comes from recognition plus the shift rule, not from a new transform integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Choosing the Sign in the Time-Shift Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $x(t+4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the plus sign in the time argument. $x(t+4)$ is an advance, so rewrite it as $x(t-(-4))$ and read the shift amount as $t_0=-4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-t_0) \\leftrightarrow e^{-j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega), \\quad t_0=-4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the negative shift into the rule. That turns the factor into $e^{-j\\omega(-4)}=e^{+j4\\omega}$, so the sign flips because the signal moved left in time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+4) \\leftrightarrow e^{+j4\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the correct factor for an advance is the positive exponent. The output keeps the base spectrum $X(j\\omega)$ and only changes the sign in the shift factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Applying Time Shifting to a Shifted Sum of Known Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $3x(t-1)-2x(t+2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a weighted sum of two shifted copies of the same base signal. Use linearity first, then apply the time-shift rule to each term on its own.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3x(t-1)-2x(t+2) \\leftrightarrow 3\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t-1)\\}-2\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t+2)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first term is a delay of $1$, so it picks up $e^{-j\\omega}$. The second term is an advance of $2$, so it picks up $e^{+j2\\omega}$; the coefficient $-2$ stays attached to that term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3x(t-1) \\leftrightarrow 3e^{-j\\omega}X(j\\omega), \\quad -2x(t+2) \\leftrightarrow -2e^{+j2\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine the transformed pieces after the fact. Both terms share the same base spectrum $X(j\\omega)$, so the final answer is just the sum of the two shifted contributions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "G(j\\omega)=\\left(3e^{-j\\omega}-2e^{+j2\\omega}\\right)X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
